      "source": "Candi Gedong Songo, Shiva temple floor plan, Semarang Indonesia",
      "description": "This is a JPEG format plan and architectural drawing of a historic Indian temple or monument. An alternate SVG format (scalable vector graphics) version of this file – for web graphics, design studies, print, dynamic and interactive applications – has also been uploaded to wikimedia commons.\nThe drawing:\n\nGedong Songo complex of temples is located on the hillside of Mountain Ungaran. It has nine temples grouped in five Gedong (lit. complex of temples). Gedong-1 and Gedong-2 are on the eastern side of the hill, Gedong-3 and Gedong-4 to the north side, and Gedong-5 to the west of the hill. This is a historic volcanic region of central Java, and these temples are built from the volcanic rocks cut and carved into reliefs. These are Shiva temples.\nThis is the floor plan of one of the nine Gedong Songo temples.\nThe temple's architectural plan follows the square and circle principle found in historic Sanskrit texts.\nFor further information, see N.J. Krom and F.D.K. Bosch (1914-1915), Inventarisatie der Hindoe-oudheden op den grondslag; R.D.M. Verbeek (1915), Oudheden van Java samengesteld op het Oudheidkundig Bureau; N.J. Krom (1923), Inleiding tot de Hindoe-Javaansche Kunst. For archival photos, see the collection of Oudheidkundige Dienst.\nGPS location of the monument:\n7° 12′ 30.6″ S, 110° 20′ 30.4″ E View this and other nearby images on: OpenStreetMap -7.208500;  110.341778\nThe relative scale and relative dimensions in this architectural drawing are close to the actual but neither exact nor complete. The plan illustrates the design and layout, but some intricate details or parts of the temple may not be shown. In cases where exact measurements were not feasible, the drawing uses best approximations and rounds the best measurements feasible.\nNote: Please do not overwrite this file. To modify or correct or load a new version, please upload a new separate file and link the new other version(s) to this file as recommended by wikimedia commons guidelines.",
